andidate_alpha vehicle_unit_gamma would still be true, which would conflict with allocating to shift_candidate_beta (precondition requires vehicle_available and not vehicle_allocated ?candidate). Actually allocate_vehicle_unit precondition: (candidate_created ?candidate) (not (vehicle_allocated ?candidate)) (vehicle_available ?vehicle_unit). It does not check candidate_vehicle_linked. So we could still allocate vehicle_unit_gamma to shift_candidate_beta even if shift_candidate_alpha still has candidate_vehicle_linked? That would cause two candidates linked to same vehicle unit, which may be allowed? But likely not intended; but there is no explicit constraint preventing multiple candidate_vehicle_linked. However there is predicate vehicle_allocated ?candidate indicating allocation; after dispatch, vehicle_allocated ?candidate remains? The action does not remove vehicle_allocated. Actually allocate_vehicle_unit effect includes (vehicle_allocated ?candidate). The dispatch actions do not affect vehicle_allocated. So vehicle_allocated remains true for shift_candidate_alpha after dispatch. That would violate precondition for allocate_vehicle_unit to shift_candidate_beta because it requires (not (vehicle_allocated ?candidate)). So we need to make vehicle_allocated shift_candidate_alpha false. There's no action to deallocate vehicle. However maybe we can release vehicle via something else? Not defined. Could we use detach_task_profile? No. There's no action to unlink vehicle. So perhaps we cannot change vehicle allocation after allocated. Thus we must keep vehicle_unit_gamma with shift_candidate_alpha and not allocate to shift_candidate_beta. But goal requires vehicle_allocated shift_candidate_beta. So maybe we need to allocate a different vehicle unit to shift_candidate_beta, like vehicle_unit_alpha or beta? But goal also wants vehicle_available vehicle_unit_alpha. So we need vehicle_unit_alpha free. Could allocate vehicle_unit_beta to shift_candidate_beta? But goal does 